---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout id="Memory" title="Memory">

  <h1>Memory</h1>

  <h2>Caching</h2>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <p>
        Memory Isolation: The translation cache and Code Morphing software reside in a dedicated memory region completely isolated from x86 code.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Initialization & Sizing: Code Morphing software copies itself from ROM into DRAM at startup for faster execution. The cache size can be configured at boot time or dynamically adjusted by the OS.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Locality of Reference: Capitalizes on high code repetition rates in real-world workloads. Once an x86 block is translated, subsequent executions hit in the cache and run at full VLIW hardware speed without re-translation.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Alias Hardware</h2>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <p>
        Load-and-Protect: When the translator speculatively schedules a load before a store, it converts it to a load-and-protect instruction that logs the target memory address and region size.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Store-Under-Alias-Mask: Subsequent stores check against protected address ranges using alias-mask hardware.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Exception & Recovery: If a store overwrites speculatively loaded data, the CPU triggers a hardware exception, allowing the runtime software to rollback and correct execution safely.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Coping with self-modifying code</h2>

    <ol>
    <li>
      <p>
        The Challenge: x86 instructions in memory may be overwritten during process loading or by self-modifying code, risking stale translation execution.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Page Write-Protection: When a block is translated, the underlying MMU sets a hidden "translated" bit on the corresponding x86 memory page, marking it as write-protected.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Invalidation & Adaptive Strategy: Attempts to write to a protected page trigger an alert to invalidate affected translations. The runtime system adaptively employs more refined invalidation strategies as it learns the application's execution patterns.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>

</Layout>